{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789397647,"nonce":"Yz/wjvir7ItpJhExAl4cE5/pU5A=","title":"Python: a generator's environment and the boxes it yields are never released","message":"pressure/generators.py, `for v in three(i): total += v` per step, grew\nby about 6.6 KB a step before the fiber was freed (63 MB at 2,000 steps\nto 1.3 GB at 200,000), and about 430 bytes a step after (32 MB to 117\nMB). The stack was the bulk; the rest is what this issue tracks.\n\nPer step now: the environment list (header, buffer, the box holding it,\nand a box per argument) built by `start_generator`, and one box per\nyielded value, allocated in the fiber body and handed to the consumer\nthrough `krio_fiber_yield`.\n\nThe fiber is freed only where the consumer can tell it owns it: a `for`\nor a draining call (`list`, `sum`, ...) whose generator expression is\nitself the start (`zb_fiber_start`), decided by `is_generator_start`.\nA generator held in a variable is not freed.\n\nWhat would fix the rest: freeing the environment header, buffer and box\nwith the fiber (the runtime would have to hand the environment back at\n`krio_fiber_free`, and cells inside must stay), and releasing a yielded\nbox at the end of each consumer iteration when the loop body keeps\nnothing of the loop variable, by the same analysis a handler uses for\nits exception (`scope::handler_keeps_exception`, widened to arithmetic\nand comparison operands and subscript reads).","files":null}]}